WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
This is a Galton Board. Maybe you've seen&nbsp;
one before. It's a popular demonstration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:10.080
of how even when a single event is chaotic and&nbsp;
random, with an effectively unknowable outcome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.220
it's still possible to make precise&nbsp;
statements about a large number of events,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:18.360
namely how the relative proportions for&nbsp;
many different outcomes are distributed.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.820
More specifically, the Galton board&nbsp;
illustrates one of the most prominent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:27.300
distributions in all of probability,&nbsp;
known as the Normal distribution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:32.700
more colloquially known as a Bell curve, and&nbsp;
also called a Gaussian distribution. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.600
a very specific function to describe this&nbsp;
distribution. Very pretty. We'll get into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.380
it later, but right now, I just want to&nbsp;
emphasize how the normal distribution is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:45.180
as the name suggests, very common. It shows&nbsp;
up in a lot of seemingly unrelated contexts.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:50.340
If you were to take a large number of people&nbsp;
who sit in a similar demographic and plot their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:55.620
heights, those heights tend to follow a normal&nbsp;
distribution. If you look at a large swath of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:00.180
very big natural numbers and you ask how many&nbsp;
distinct prime factors does each one of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.980
numbers have, the answers will very closely&nbsp;
track with a certain normal distribution.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:09.840
Now, our topic for today is one of the crown&nbsp;
jewels in all of probability theory. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:13.920
one of the key facts that explains why&nbsp;
this distribution is as common as it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.660
known as the Central Limit Theorem. This&nbsp;
lesson is meant to go back to the basics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.780
giving you the fundamentals on what&nbsp;
the Central Limit Theorem is saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:24.720
what normal distributions are, and&nbsp;
I want to assume minimal background.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.520
We're going to go decently deep into it, but&nbsp;
after this, I'd still like to go deeper and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:35.040
explain why the theorem is true, why the function&nbsp;
underlying the normal distribution has the very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:41.760
specific form that it does, why that formula has&nbsp;
a pi in it, and, most fun, why those last two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:45.600
facts are actually more related than a lot&nbsp;
of traditional explanations would suggest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:51.720
That second lesson is also meant to be the follow&nbsp;
on to the convolutions video that I promised. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:55.500
there's a lot of interrelated topics here.&nbsp;
But right now, back to the fundamentals. I'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:59.280
like to kick things off with an overly&nbsp;
simplified model of the Galton board.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:05.100
In this model, we will assume that each&nbsp;
ball falls directly onto a certain central&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.660
peg and then it has a 50 - 50 probability&nbsp;
of bouncing to the left or to the right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.920
and we'll think of each of those outcomes as&nbsp;
either adding one or subtracting one from its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.960
position. Once one of those is chosen, we&nbsp;
make the highly unrealistic assumption that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:23.220
it happens to land dead on in the middle&nbsp;
of the peg adjacent below it, where again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:27.120
it'll be faced with the same 50 - 50 choice&nbsp;
of bouncing to the left or to the right.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:31.500
For the one I'm showing on screen, there are&nbsp;
five different rows of pegs. So our little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:35.100
hopping ball makes five different&nbsp;
random choices between plus one and&nbsp;&nbsp;

