00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:01.440
The basic idea of the central limit theorem&nbsp;
is that if you increase the size of that sum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:04.980
for example, here that would mean&nbsp;
increasing the number of rows of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:09.840
pegs for each ball to bounce off, then&nbsp;
the distribution that describes where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:13.620
that sum is going to fall looks&nbsp;
more and more like a bell curve.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:18.480
Here, it's actually worth taking a&nbsp;
moment to write down that general idea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:24.240
The setup is that we have a random variable&nbsp;
and that's basically shorthand for a random&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:29.160
process where each outcome of that process is&nbsp;
associated with some number. We'll call that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:33.900
