On our Galton board, that looks like letting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:57.240
ball bounce off multiple different pegs on&nbsp;
its way down to the bottom, and in the case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:00.900
of a die, you might imagine rolling many&nbsp;
different dice and adding up the results.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:06.120
The claim of the central limit theorem is that&nbsp;
as you let the size of that sum get bigger and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:11.160
bigger, then the distribution of that sum, how&nbsp;
likely it is to fall into different possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:17.280
