the expected value for a die roll, but what's&nbsp;
much less obvious and what the central limit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:51.540 --> 00:27:56.460
theorem lets you compute is how close to that&nbsp;
expected value you'll reasonably find yourself.

00:27:57.420 --> 00:28:00.600
In particular, it's worth your time&nbsp;
to take a moment mulling over what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:00.600 --> 00:28:03.060
the standard deviation for this empirical average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.060 --> 00:28:07.260
is and what happens to it as you look at&nbsp;
a bigger and bigger sample of die rolls.

00:28:11.880 --> 00:28:17.580
Lastly, but probably most importantly, let's talk&nbsp;
about the assumptions that go into this theorem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:17.580 --> 00:28:22.500
The first one is that all of these variables that&nbsp;
we're adding up are independent from each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:22.500 --> 00:28:28.200
The outcome of one process doesn't influence the&nbsp;
outcome of any other process. The second is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:28.200 --> 00:28:33.120
all of these variables are drawn from the same&nbsp;
distribution. Both of these have been implicitly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:33.120 --> 00:28:37.980
assumed with our dice example. We've been treating&nbsp;
the outcome of each die roll as independent from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:37.980 --> 00:28:42.000
the outcome of all the others and we're assuming&nbsp;
that each die follows the same distribution.

00:28:42.720 --> 00:28:45.780
Sometimes in the literature you'll see&nbsp;
these two assumptions lumped together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:45.780 --> 00:28:51.240
under the initials i.i.d for Independent&nbsp;
and identically distributed. One situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:51.240 --> 00:28:55.980
where these assumptions are decidedly NOT&nbsp;
true would be the Galton board. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:55.980 --> 00:29:01.440
think about it - is it the case that the way a&nbsp;
ball bounces off of one of the pegs is independent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:01.440 --> 00:29:05.760
from how it's going to bounce off the next peg?&nbsp;
Absolutely not. Depending on the last bounce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:05.760 --> 00:29:09.720
it's coming in with a completely different&nbsp;
trajectory. And is it the case that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.720 --> 00:29:15.600
distribution of possible outcomes off of each peg&nbsp;
are the same for each peg that it hits? Again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:20.100
almost certainly not. Maybe it hits one peg&nbsp;
glancing to the left, meaning the outcomes are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:20.100 --> 00:29:23.820
hugely skewed in that direction and then&nbsp;
hits the next one glancing to the right.

00:29:25.740 --> 00:29:28.860
When I made all those simplifying&nbsp;
assumptions in the opening example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:28.860 --> 00:29:33.720
it wasn't just to make this easier to think&nbsp;
about. It's also that those assumptions were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:33.720 --> 00:29:37.260
necessary for this to actually be an&nbsp;
example of the central limit theorem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:37.920 --> 00:29:40.980
Nevertheless, it seems to be true&nbsp;
that for the real Galton board,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:40.980 --> 00:29:45.420
despite violating both of these, a normal&nbsp;
distribution does kind of come about...

00:29:46.320 --> 00:29:49.740
Part of the reason might be that there&nbsp;
are generalizations of the theorem beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:49.740 --> 00:29:53.820
the scope of this video that relax these&nbsp;
assumptions, especially the second one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:54.360 --> 00:29:58.920
But I do want to caution you against the fact&nbsp;
that many times, people seem to assume that a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:58.920 --> 00:30:03.000
variable is normally distributed even when&nbsp;
there's no actual justification to do so.

00:30:04.140 --> 00:30:08.520
The third assumption is actually fairly subtle.&nbsp;
It's that the variance we've been computing for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:08.520 --> 00:30:13.980
these variables is finite. This was never an issue&nbsp;
for the dice example because there were only six&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:13.980 --> 00:30:18.300
possible outcomes, but in certain situations&nbsp;
where you have an infinite set of outcomes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:18.300 --> 00:30:22.380
when you go to compute the variance, the&nbsp;
sum ends up diverging off to infinity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:23.340 --> 00:30:27.240
These can be perfectly valid probability&nbsp;
distributions and they do come up in practice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:27.240 --> 00:30:32.340
but in those situations, as you consider adding&nbsp;
many different instantiations of that variable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:32.340 --> 00:30:37.140
and letting that sum approach infinity, even&nbsp;
if the first two assumptions hold, it is very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:37.140 --> 00:30:41.160
much a possibility that the thing you tender&nbsp;
towards is not actually a normal distribution.

00:30:42.120 --> 00:30:44.040
If you've understood everything up to this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:44.040 --> 00:30:48.780
you now have a very strong foundation in what the&nbsp;
central limit theorem is all about. And next up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:48.780 --> 00:30:52.560
I'd like to explain why it is that this&nbsp;
particular function is the thing that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:52.560 --> 00:30:56.100
tend towards and why it has a pi in&nbsp;
it - what it has to do with circles.

